## Proof

**Notation.** Let $\mathcal{S}$ be the class of all sets $S$ of ordinals that admit a representation
$$S = \bigcup_{n < \omega} F_n,$$
where each $F_n$ is a finite set of ordinals. Let
$$\mathcal{T} = \{\,\operatorname{otp}(S, \in) : S \in \mathcal{S}\,\}$$
be the set of order types (each $S$, being a set of ordinals, is well-ordered by $\in$, so its order type is an ordinal). We prove that $\sup \mathcal{T} = \omega_1$.

---

### Upper bound: $\sup \mathcal{T} \le \omega_1$.

Let $S \in \mathcal{S}$, so $S = \bigcup_{n<\omega} F_n$ with each $F_n$ finite. A countable union of finite sets is countable, so $S$ is countable. Since $S \subseteq \mathrm{Ord}$, the membership relation $\in$ well-orders $S$, and $\operatorname{otp}(S) = \alpha$ for some ordinal $\alpha$. The bijection between $S$ and $\alpha$ (the unique order-isomorphism) witnesses $|\alpha| = |S| \le \aleph_0$, so $\alpha$ is a countable ordinal, i.e., $\alpha < \omega_1$. Hence $\mathcal{T} \subseteq \omega_1$, giving $\sup \mathcal{T} \le \omega_1$.

---

### Lower bound: $\sup \mathcal{T} \ge \omega_1$.

We show every countable ordinal belongs to $\mathcal{T}$, i.e., $\omega_1 \subseteq \mathcal{T}$.

Let $\alpha < \omega_1$ be any countable ordinal. Then $\alpha$ is a countable set of ordinals (its elements are precisely the ordinals below $\alpha$). Choose an enumeration $\alpha = \{\beta_n : n < \omega\}$ (possible since $\alpha$ is countable) and set $F_n = \{\beta_n\}$ for each $n < \omega$. Each $F_n$ is a finite (singleton) set of ordinals, and
$$\alpha = \bigcup_{n < \omega} F_n.$$
Moreover, $\alpha$ well-ordered by $\in$ has order type $\operatorname{otp}(\alpha) = \alpha$ (a standard fact: the identity map on $\alpha$ is the unique order-isomorphism from $(\alpha, \in)$ to $(\alpha, \in)$). Therefore $\alpha \in \mathcal{T}$.

Since every $\alpha < \omega_1$ lies in $\mathcal{T}$, we have $\omega_1 \subseteq \mathcal{T}$, and consequently $\sup \mathcal{T} \ge \sup \omega_1 = \omega_1$.

---

### Conclusion.

Combining both bounds:
$$\sup \mathcal{T} = \omega_1.$$

The set of order types of countable unions of finite sets of ordinals is exactly $\omega_1$ (the set of all countable ordinals), and its least upper bound is $\omega_1$, the first uncountable ordinal. This holds in ZFC since the argument uses only the axioms needed to define $\omega_1$ (Replacement, Power Set, and the standard construction of the Hartogs number of $\omega$).

